1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,156
This program looks correct.

2
00:00:02,276 --> 00:00:06,018
It also knocks over the glass in 19 of 20 random layouts.

3
00:00:06,918 --> 00:00:10,079
Telling a robot what to do still means writing a program,

4
00:00:10,079 --> 00:00:13,185
and the person who knows the task usually can't read it.

5
00:00:13,305 --> 00:00:15,593
Show and Tell Robotics changes that.

6
00:00:16,093 --> 00:00:21,207
You show the task once on video, and say the rule in one sentence: sort by color,

7
00:00:21,207 --> 00:00:23,480
but blue things stay where they are.

8
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:26,928
Here, a simulator render stands in for the phone clip.

9
00:00:27,428 --> 00:00:29,892
Muse Wonder names the objects on the table.

10
00:00:30,292 --> 00:00:35,587
SAM 3.1 tracks each one: what moved, in what order, and what stayed put.

11
00:00:36,087 --> 00:00:40,752
Before any code exists, Muse Wonder watches the video and writes the tests:

12
00:00:40,752 --> 00:00:44,670
red ends in the left bin, green in the right, blue never moves.

13
00:00:44,790 --> 00:00:47,103
You read them, edit them, approve.

14
00:00:47,503 --> 00:00:50,926
Then it writes a short program out of tested robot skills.

15
00:00:51,326 --> 00:00:56,403
It runs on 20 randomized scenes in MuJoCo, with a simulated Franka arm.

16
00:00:56,523 --> 00:00:59,229
This one passes all 20 on the first try.

17
00:00:59,729 --> 00:01:02,098
So what happens when a scene fails?

18
00:01:02,218 --> 00:01:06,784
In this recorded run, from the sentence alone, 19 scenes pass.

19
00:01:07,184 --> 00:01:10,498
In scene 6004, the program stops after the lift:

20
00:01:10,498 --> 00:01:15,744
its detour around a blue cube is out of reach, so the arm lets go and parks.

21
00:01:16,444 --> 00:01:20,743
Muse Wonder gets the failed checks, the error and eight frames from that scene.

22
00:01:21,143 --> 00:01:22,843
Its diagnosis, word for word:

23
00:01:22,843 --> 00:01:28,531
Avoiding the blue cubes forced an unreachable over-the-blue waypoint while carrying the red cube,

24
00:01:28,531 --> 00:01:30,759
aborting the program before placement.

25
00:01:31,259 --> 00:01:32,653
The patch drops the detour:

26
00:01:32,653 --> 00:01:36,008
the blue cubes are low enough for the arm's normal travel height.

27
00:01:36,508 --> 00:01:40,343
All 20 scenes run again. 20/20: verified.

28
00:01:40,843 --> 00:01:46,629
Every scene also gets a second judge: SAM 3.1, looking only at the rendered video.

29
00:01:46,749 --> 00:01:51,615
It agreed with the simulator on all 120 checks, the failure included.

30
00:01:51,615 --> 00:01:53,590
The simulator still decides.

31
00:01:54,090 --> 00:01:58,887
Why the video? From the sentence alone, four of six tasks match our reference:

32
00:01:58,887 --> 00:02:02,638
where the sentence doesn't name the bin, Wonder has to guess.

33
00:02:02,758 --> 00:02:06,506
The run you just saw is a miss: it guessed the middle bin for green.

34
00:02:06,626 --> 00:02:12,294
With a stand-in clip, six of six match, on the first try. Human clips are next.

35
00:02:12,794 --> 00:02:18,081
And the glass? On that same layout, Muse Wonder's program lifts above it and goes around.

36
00:02:18,201 --> 00:02:21,768
Every program it has written for that task avoids the glass.

37
00:02:22,268 --> 00:02:26,370
Muse Wonder does four jobs here, and SAM 3.1 does two.

38
00:02:26,490 --> 00:02:31,095
Generated code runs in a sandbox, pass or fail comes from physics,

39
00:02:31,095 --> 00:02:35,143
and a verified task costs one to two dollars in API calls.

40
00:02:35,643 --> 00:02:41,567
Integration generally runs four to five times the cost of the robot, says MIT's Julie Shah.

41
00:02:41,687 --> 00:02:47,160
We want the person who knows the job to change it: one demonstration, one sentence,

42
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:48,215
one test report.

43
00:02:48,715 --> 00:02:52,491
It runs in simulation today. Next is a pilot on a real arm,

44
00:02:52,491 --> 00:02:55,243
through the same skills and the same tests.
